{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"piercing-grained-elver","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`commit_impact.upstream_branch_refs()` builds its set of upstream refs by including only refs whose name ends in `/<current-branch>`. When the local checkout's current branch name differs from the branch the remote commit lives on, the include-upstream scan silently ignores the correct remote ref — **even after a successful fetch**.\n\nDiscovered incidentally during the sandbox failure census under `rebuffable-pebbly-xenurine` (`985f-0ec5-a61c-46ee`). It is the one failure in that census with no environmental component: it reproduces as an ordinary baseline defect.\n\nFailing test: `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_completion_gate.py::test_file_impact_close_fetches_before_rejecting_stale_clone`\n\nObserved shape: the copied fixture repo is on `master`, the remote commit is on `origin/main`. The gate fetches successfully, then scans upstream refs, finds nothing ending in `/master` that carries the commit, and rejects.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThe completion gate's stale-clone path exists precisely to avoid rejecting a close when the work *has* landed but the local clone had not yet seen it. `AGENTS.md` documents the remedy for that state as fetching and fast-forwarding. This defect means the fetch can succeed and the gate still reject, because the scan is looking under the wrong ref name.\n\nThe practical consequence is a false \"cannot confirm the work landed\" on any checkout whose branch name does not match the remote branch — a throwaway worktree, a clone left on `master`, or any branch naming that diverges from the upstream branch. The failure is a false negative, which is the safe direction, but it blocks legitimate closes and pushes operators toward overrides.\n\nThis is adjacent to `working-carnelian-bobwhite` (`8806-198c-e2d6-46ce`), which concerns the completion gate verifying against a stale tree. Both are defects in what tree or ref the gate considers authoritative, and they should be assessed together — a fix to one may bear on the other.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. The upstream-ref scan no longer depends on the local current-branch name matching the remote branch name.\n2. A test reproduces the original failure — fixture on one branch, remote commit on another — and fails before the fix, passes after.\n3. `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_completion_gate.py::test_file_impact_close_fetches_before_rejecting_stale_clone` passes.\n4. A determination of whether the correct behaviour is to scan all upstream refs, to resolve the configured upstream branch explicitly, or to use the ref the fetch actually updated. The choice is stated with its reasoning, since these differ when a commit exists on several branches.\n5. No regression in the gate's ability to reject a genuinely stale clone. The gate must still refuse when the work truly has not landed anywhere upstream — the fix must not convert a false negative into a false positive.\n\n## Notes\n\nThe defect is in ref *selection*, not in the fetch, which succeeds. Confirm whether the fixture's use of `master` is itself intentional in the test; if the fixture is simply wrong, the right fix may be in the test rather than the scan — but the `/<current-branch>` suffix match remains fragile either way and the criterion above should be settled regardless.","id":"9753-647b-2f85-40ef","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Close gate's upstream scan misses the remote ref when local branch name differs"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789689313554645724,"uuid":"e222b2a6-1e45-4cc4-95f6-347b34913f74"}